            <ArticleTitle>Scleroderma of the colon presenting with acute abdominal symptoms.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>511-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 66-year-old woman with long-standing systemic sclerosis presented with fever, acute abdominal pain and tenderness. She was treated with antibiotics. At laparotomy six weeks later, a narrow, stiff, friable and fibrotic right colon was found with histological features of scleroderma. The acute attack was interpreted as an ischaemic episode of the colon which later healed by fibrosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
